= Solution
{parent=i}

The <derivative> of $\tan x$ is $1/\cos^2x$. The <fundamental theorem of calculus> gives
$$
\boxed{\int_0^{\pi/4}\frac{dx}{\cos^2x}=\tan(\pi/4)-\tan0=1.}
$$

= ii
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The integrand is an <odd function>, because $x$ is odd and $\cos x$ is even. Its integral over the symmetric interval is therefore
$$
\boxed{0.}
$$

= 3
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

The denominator $D=x^2+xy+y^2=(x+y/2)^2+3y^2/4$ is positive away from the origin. The <partial derivatives> are
$$
f_x=\frac{y(y^2-x^2)}{D^2},\qquad f_y=\frac{x(x^2-y^2)}{D^2}.
$$
They both vanish exactly on
$$
\boxed{y=x\quad\text{and}\quad y=-x,\qquad(x,y)\ne(0,0).}
$$
These are lines of <stationary points>, with values $1/3$ and $-1$, respectively.

