= Solution
{parent=i}

Differentiate the sum of squares in <Cartesian coordinates>:
$$
\boxed{\nabla(r^2)=(2x,2y,2z)=2\boldsymbol r.}
$$

= ii
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <product rule for divergence> gives $\nabla\cdot(r^2\boldsymbol r)=\nabla(r^2)\cdot\boldsymbol r+r^2\nabla\cdot\boldsymbol r$. Since $\nabla\cdot\boldsymbol r=3$, the result is
$$
\boxed{\nabla\cdot(r^2\boldsymbol r)=5r^2.}
$$

= 5
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=5}

Multiply numerator and denominator by the <complex conjugate> of $1-i$:
$$
\frac{1+i}{1-i}=\frac{(1+i)^2}2=i.
$$
Thus the <real part> and <imaginary part> are respectively
$$
\boxed{0,\qquad1.}
$$

= 6
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=6}

The <characteristic polynomial> is $(1/2-\lambda)(-1/2-\lambda)-2=\lambda^2-9/4$. Its two <roots> give the <eigenvalues>
$$
\boxed{\lambda=\frac32,\quad-\frac32.}
$$

= 7
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=7}

The <Maclaurin series> $\cos x=1-x^2/2+O(x^4)$ gives $e^{\cos x}=e\exp(-x^2/2+O(x^4))$. The first two nonzero terms are therefore
$$
\boxed{e^{\cos x}=e-\frac e2x^2+O(x^4).}
$$

