= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Every set of $S$ students is equally likely to be the specified college. There are $\binom NS$ choices in total and $\binom LS$ choices consisting entirely of the specified group. Therefore the <hypergeometric distribution> gives
$$
\boxed{P(\text{all lazy})=\frac{\binom LS}{\binom NS}.}
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Choose $n$ students from the $L$ lazy students and $S-n$ from the other $N-L$. Dividing by the total number of possible college memberships gives
$$
\boxed{P(n\text{ lazy})=\frac{\binom Ln\binom{N-L}{S-n}}{\binom NS},\qquad0\leq n\leq S.}
$$
The assumptions ensure all these values are possible. This sampling is without replacement, as required by the <hypergeometric distribution>.

= b
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

By symmetry, each student in the university has probability $(S/N)(1/S)=1/N$ of being in the college and then selected. Hence the <prior probability> is
$$
\boxed{P(\text{lazy})=L/N.}
$$

= c
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The lecture attendances are independent <Bernoulli trials> with success probability $f$. Their count has the <binomial distribution>:
$$
\boxed{P(k\mid K,f)=\binom Kk f^k(1-f)^{K-k},\qquad0\leq k\leq K.}
$$

= d
{parent=11}
{scope}

= i
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Here “absent” denotes absence at both observed lectures. The assumed <independence> makes its <conditional probability>
$$
\boxed{P(\text{absent}\mid\text{lazy})=(1-1/2)^2=1/4.}
$$

= ii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For attendance probability $9/10$, absence on both independent observations has <conditional probability>
$$
\boxed{P(\text{absent}\mid\text{diligent})=(1-9/10)^2=1/100.}
$$

= iii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The <law of total probability> over the two student groups gives
$$
P(\text{absent})=\frac14\frac LN+\frac1{100}\left(1-\frac LN\right),
\qquad\boxed{P(\text{absent})=\frac{24L+N}{100N}.}
$$

= iv
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

The <prior probability> is $1/10$. Using <Bayes' theorem>, the <posterior probability> after two absences is
$$
\boxed{P(\text{lazy}\mid\text{absent})=\frac{(1/4)(1/10)}{(1/4)(1/10)+(1/100)(9/10)}=\frac{25}{34}\approx0.7353.}
$$
Thus the probability rises from **10% to about 73.5%** within the assumed attendance model. Two absences are 25 times as likely under the lazy model, overcoming the prior odds of $1:9$; they still do not make the classification certain.

= 12
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=12}

The integrand is even and decays at both infinity and zero, since $a,b>0$. On $x>0$ set $u=\sqrt a\,x-\sqrt b/x$, a strictly increasing bijection onto the whole real line. Then
$$
ax^2+b/x^2=u^2+2\sqrt{ab},\qquad
x=\frac{u+\sqrt{u^2+4\sqrt{ab}}}{2\sqrt a},\qquad
\frac{dx}{du}=\frac1{2\sqrt a}\left(1+\frac u{\sqrt{u^2+4\sqrt{ab}}}\right).
$$
Using evenness for the negative half-axis gives
$$
I(a,b)=\frac{e^{-\sqrt{ab}}}{\sqrt a}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}e^{-u^2/2}\left(1+\frac u{\sqrt{u^2+4\sqrt{ab}}}\right)du.
$$
The second summand is an integrable <odd function>, so its integral vanishes. The remaining <Gaussian integral> gives
$$
\boxed{I(a,b)=\sqrt{\frac{2\pi}a}\,e^{-\sqrt{ab}}.}
$$
For the second integral use $x=1/t$ separately on each half-axis. The factor $x^{-2}dx$ becomes $-dt$, and reversing the transformed limits yields $J(a,b)=I(b,a)$. Thus
$$
\boxed{J(a,b)=\sqrt{\frac{2\pi}b}\,e^{-\sqrt{ab}}.}
$$
These identities are instances of the <Gaussian integral with an inverse-square term>. Splitting at zero before either <change of variables> avoids treating a discontinuous transformation as a single bijection.

