The <characteristic equation> $(r-3)(r-4)=0$ gives complementary terms $Ce^{3x}+De^{4x}$. A linear <particular solution> is $12x+7$, since its substitution gives $144x$. The initial conditions give $C+D=-7$ and $3C+4D=-12$, hence
$$
\boxed{y=12x+7-16e^{3x}+9e^{4x}.}
$$

= ii
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The complementary <characteristic roots> are $-1,-2$. Substituting $A\sin x+B\cos x$ into the differential operator gives $(A-3B)\sin x+(3A+B)\cos x$, so a <particular solution> is $\sin x-3\cos x$. The initial conditions then require $C+D=3$ and $C+2D=1$, giving
$$
\boxed{y=\sin x-3\cos x+5e^{-x}-2e^{-2x}.}
$$

= iii
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The repeated <characteristic root> is $-1$, matching the exponential forcing. Put $y=e^{-x}w$; then $y''+2y'+y=e^{-x}w''$, so $w''=8$. The zero initial conditions give $w(0)=w'(0)=0$, hence
$$
\boxed{y=4x^2e^{-x}.}
$$
The extra quadratic factor is the <resonance> associated with the double root.

= 14
{parent=Paper 2}
