= a
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Put $\xi=x-vt$ and $U(\xi)=A\operatorname{sech}^2\xi$. For $A\ne0$, differentiation gives $U''=4U-6U^2/A$ and therefore $U^{(3)}=4U'-(12/A)UU'$. The <Korteweg-De Vries equation> reduces to
$$
(4-v)U'-\left(6+\frac{12}A\right)UU'=0.
$$
Since $U$ is nonconstant, both coefficients must vanish. Thus the nonzero <solitary wave> is
$$
\boxed{A=-2,\qquad v=4,\qquad u=-2\operatorname{sech}^2(x-4t).}
$$
There is also the trivial solution $A=0$, for which $v$ has no effect. The negative amplitude follows from the minus sign of the nonlinear term; it agrees with the <sech-squared solitary wave of the KdV equation>.

\Image[paper-2-negative-kdv-soliton.png]
{title=A negative solitary wave translates to the right without changing its profile}

= b
{parent=14}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Differentiate the equation for $f$ with respect to $x$, commuting mixed <partial derivatives> for a sufficiently smooth solution. With $y=f_x$ this gives
$$
f_{xt}+f_xf_{xx}=kf_{xxx},\qquad\boxed{y_t+yy_x=ky_{xx}.}
$$
This is the <viscous Burgers equation> when $k>0$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For $k\ne0$, the <chain rule> gives $g_t=-f_tg/(2k)$ and $g_{xx}=(-f_{xx}/(2k)+f_x^2/(4k^2))g$. Consequently
$$
g_t-kg_{xx}=-\frac g{2k}\left(f_t+\frac12f_x^2-kf_{xx}\right)=0.
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{g_t=kg_{xx}.}
$$
This is the potential form of the <Cole-Hopf transformation>, with $y=-2k\,g_x/g$. For $k>0$ it is a forward <diffusion equation>; at $k=0$ the exponential transformation is undefined, while negative $k$ gives a backward heat equation.

