Integrating the first component in $x$ gives $\phi=x^3y+h(y,z)$. Matching the second requires $h_y=-2yz^2$, so $h=-y^2z^2+k(z)$. Matching the third gives $k'=0$. Thus the field is <conservative>, with <scalar potential>
$$
\boxed{\phi=x^3y-y^2z^2+C.}
$$
Direct differentiation verifies $\boldsymbol F_1=\nabla\phi$ on all of $\mathbb R^3$.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <curl> is $\nabla\times\boldsymbol F_2=(2z,3z^2,0)$, which is not identically zero. A gradient of a smooth <scalar potential> has zero <curl>, so $\boldsymbol F_2$ is **not conservative** on $\mathbb R^3$.

= b
{parent=15}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

On the first segment, $x=z=0$, so both <line integrals> vanish. On the second, $y=1,z=0$, giving contributions $\int_0^23x^2dx=8$ and $\int_0^22x\,dx=4$. On the last, $x=2,y=1$, the contributions are $\int_0^3-2z\,dz=-9$ and $\int_0^3-6z^2dz=-54$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\int\boldsymbol F_1\cdot d\boldsymbol r=-1,\qquad\int\boldsymbol F_2\cdot d\boldsymbol r=-50.}
$$
The first also equals $\phi(B)-\phi(A)=8-9=-1$, as the <fundamental theorem for line integrals> predicts.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <parametric curve> runs from $t=0$ to $t=1$, with $\boldsymbol r'=(2,3t^2,6t)$. Substitution into the two <line integrals> gives
$$
\boldsymbol F_1(\boldsymbol r(t))\cdot\boldsymbol r'=48t^5-90t^9,\qquad
\boldsymbol F_2(\boldsymbol r(t))\cdot\boldsymbol r'=20t^4-351t^6.
$$
Hence
$$
\boxed{\int\boldsymbol F_1\cdot d\boldsymbol r=-1,\qquad\int\boldsymbol F_2\cdot d\boldsymbol r=-\frac{323}7.}
$$
The first is unchanged because the field is <conservative>. The two different answers for the second exhibit its path dependence, consistent with its nonzero <curl>.

