The <permutation matrix> acts by $A(x,y,z)^T=(y,z,x)^T$. Applying this cyclic permutation three times returns every <vector> to itself, proving $A^3=I$. Explicitly $A^2=\begin{pmatrix}0&0&1\\1&0&0\\0&1&0\end{pmatrix}$ and multiplication once more gives the <identity matrix>.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Using $A^3=I$, the matrix power reduces modulo three:
$$
\boxed{A^{3n+2}=A^2=\begin{pmatrix}0&0&1\\1&0&0\\0&1&0\end{pmatrix}.}
$$

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Since $A^2A=A^3=I=AA^2$, the <inverse matrix> is
$$
\boxed{(A^2)^{-1}=A.}
$$

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Here $A^{3n+1}=A$, so its <inverse matrix> is
$$
\boxed{(A^{3n+1})^{-1}=A^2.}
$$

= b
{parent=16}
